<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/advanced/decolonisation-postcolonial-world">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Decolonisation is the story of <strong>empires ending — and of what they left behind</strong>.
    Between 1945 and 1975 more than eighty new nations replaced colonial rule, redrawing the
    political map faster than any event except the world wars; the United Nations grew from 51
    members in 1945 to nearly 140 by 1975. But the deeper subject is the <em>aftermath</em>:
    the borders, languages, armies, constitutions, debts and diasporas that empire bequeathed,
    and the arguments — about development, culture, memory and restitution — that remain
    unresolved in today's headlines. Reading this history in one direction only, as the
    inevitable end of an obviously doomed system, is the first trap; empire seemed durable to
    its administrators until very late. The second trap is reading it as a tidy success story:
    self-determination was a genuine triumph, and its inheritances were genuinely mixed. Both
    truths organize everything below.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="independence, its modes, and the inheritances" widget="equationStep" config={decolCfg} />

  <h2>The waves of independence</h2>
  <p>
    Decolonisation came in waves, each with its own logic. The first, 1776–1826, emptied the
    Americas: thirteen British colonies became the United States, and by 1826 Spain's mainland
    empire was gone in the wake of Napoleon's invasion — an example later Asian and African
    nationalists studied closely. The great wave followed the Second World War, and its timing
    is the war's signature: metropoles were financially exhausted, their armies had been beaten
    by Asian forces (the fall of Singapore in 1942 punctured European invincibility), tens of
    thousands of colonial soldiers had fought for freedoms they were then denied, and both
    superpowers — for opposite reasons — preached self-determination. India and Pakistan went in
    1947; Indonesia wrenched independence from the Dutch in 1945–49; Ghana led sub-Saharan
    Africa in 1957; and 1960 alone brought seventeen African states — "the Year of Africa". The
    end dates varied with the cost calculus: where white settler populations were numerous
    (Algeria, Kenya, Zimbabwe, South Africa), the metropole fought hardest and longest, because
    administrators could not be repatriated the way administrators could. The United Nations
    itself grew from 51 members in 1945 to more than 140 by the mid-1970s — the fastest
    expansion of the club of sovereign states in history.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the European empires dismantled themselves voluntarily,
    out of enlightened decency." The timetable was driven at least as much by pressure as by
    principle: the Indian National Congress's mass movements, the 1945–49 Indonesian and
    Indochinese wars, the Mau Mau emergency in Kenya (1952–60) and the Algerian war each
    raised the price of holding on, and Britain's 1968 East-of-Suez withdrawal followed a
    currency crisis, not a conversion. Historians describe a negotiation forced by
    nationalists, war debt and superpower disapproval — grants of flags at ceremonies were the
    last act of a much longer drama.
  </p>

  <h2>Partition and the inherited state</h2>
  <p>
    Independence delivered a state as well as a flag, and the state's borders and institutions
    were colonial artefacts. The sharpest case is the 1947 partition of British India: the
    Radcliffe Line, drawn in five weeks by a lawyer who had never been there, split Punjab and
    Bengal — and somewhere around fifteen million people crossed the new borders in both
    directions amid communal violence that killed, by most careful estimates, several hundred
    thousand to as many as a million; its unresolved core, Kashmir, has fuelled wars since.
    Africa inherited a different version of the same problem: the Berlin Conference (1884–85)
    and subsequent treaties drew boundaries by imperial convenience — rivers, minerals,
    railway gauges — slicing some communities among several states and fusing rivals into
    others. The sequel is instructive and often misread: newly independent states
    <em>chose</em> to keep those borders — the Organisation of African Unity pledged border
    inviolability in 1964 precisely to prevent a continent-wide war of revisions — so the
    conflicts that followed (Biafra's secession attempt, 1967–70; Rwanda's genocide, 1994;
    Sudan's split, 2011) were mostly wars <em>within</em> the inherited states about who
    controlled them, rather than wars <em>between</em> states about where the lines lay. The
    borders were not drawn randomly; they were drawn deliberately for someone else's purposes,
    which is a different and more useful indictment.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "arbitrary colonial borders caused Africa's wars." The borders
    were unjust, but the causation runs more carefully: African states chose to keep them — the
    OAU's 1964 pledge was itself a deliberate peace-keeping choice — and most post-colonial
    conflicts have been civil wars over state power rather than interstate wars over the lines
    on the map. The fuel came from what the inherited states contained: competitive
    regionalisms engineered by divide-and-rule, armies politicised by liberation struggle or
    coup, Cold War patrons arming clients, and commodity rents worth fighting for. Borders set
    the stage; they did not write the play.
  </p>

  <h2>Flag independence: neocolonialism and the economics</h2>
  <p>
    Kwame Nkrumah — Ghana's first president, and the man who made "Seek ye first the political
    kingdom" the era's slogan — coined the era's sharpest concept in 1965:
    <strong>neocolonialism</strong>, sovereignty in form with leverage still exercised from
    outside. The mechanisms were structural. Most new states exported one or two primary
    commodities — Ghana cocoa, Chile copper, Zaire cobalt — whose prices, set in metropolitan
    markets, trended against manufactured imports; the colonial division of labour persisted
    after the flag changed. The 1970s oil shocks and commodity slumps drove borrowing; the
    1980s debt crisis delivered the levers to creditors, as IMF and World Bank structural
    adjustment programmes traded loans for privatisation, cuts and export-drive policies —
    with results (growth restoration versus shredded health and education budgets) debated
    ever since. Currency arrangements made the dependence visible: fourteen African states
    kept the CFA franc, pegged to the franc and (until reforms) partly deposited with the
    French treasury — monetary sovereignty outsourced. The postcolonial economics debate is
    ultimately about agency: dependency theorists (Frank, Wallerstein) read the world economy
    as a core–periphery system that reproduces underdevelopment, while institutional economists
    both agree the starting
    line was not fair; they differ on who holds the relay baton now. The numbers are concrete:
    in the mid-1970s copper supplied the great majority of Zambia's export earnings, so the
    1975 commodity slump turned a proud new state into an indebted one within a decade — no
    corruption required, only a one-commodity economy meeting a world price it did not set.
  </p>

  <h2>Non-alignment: the third path</h2>
  <p>
    The Cold War offered the new states a menu with two items, and they declined it. Twenty-nine
    Asian and African states met at Bandung, Indonesia, in April 1955 — the first gathering of
    formerly colonised nations without their colonisers in the room — and out of it grew the
    Non-Aligned Movement, formally founded in Belgrade in 1961 and grown to some 120 members at
    its peak, the largest voting bloc in the UN outside the Security Council's permanent five.
    Non-alignment was not neutrality of conscience: Nasser took Soviet arms for the Aswan Dam
    after the West refused them, India took aid from both blocs, and Cuba fought in Angola. It
    was a strategy for converting the Cold War's bidding war into leverage — and its partial
    successes (the Suez humiliation of Britain and France in 1956 showed the old powers could
    no longer act alone) and failures (détente in the 1970s was largely negotiated over the
    Global South's head, and NAM summits spent as much time on debt and apartheid as on
    superpower manoeuvring) are the honest ledger of what a "third force" could and could not
    do against two armed camps.
  </p>

  <h2>The postcolonial turn: writing back</h2>
  <p>
    Alongside the political and economic stories runs an intellectual one: the colonised
    analysing the coloniser's account of them. Frantz Fanon's <em>The Wretched of the Earth</em>
    (1961), written as the Algerian war burned, argued colonialism deforms coloniser and
    colonised alike and that liberation is psychological as much as legal. Edward Said's
    <em>Orientalism</em> (1978) showed how the West's scholarly and artistic image of "the
    East" — exotic, static, childlike — was built <em>as an instrument of rule</em>, moving
    the argument from history departments into literature, sociology and politics. Gayatri
    Spivak's 1988 essay "Can the Subaltern Speak?" pushed further: even the sympathetic
    archive mostly records the colonised through intermediaries, so recovering their voice is
    a methodological problem, not just an archival one. Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o's <em>Decolonising
    the Mind</em> (1986) made the language question central — why should a Kenyan novel be
    written in English? — and the current generation is executing his programme in stone and
    policy: the Rhodes Must Fall movement (Cape Town, 2015), the renaming of streets and
    universities, and the slow return of the Benin Bronzes from European museums are the same
    argument, continuing in institutions rather than manifestos.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "postcolonialism is a literary mood, not a field with
    consequences." It is an analytic lens with hard policy stakes: development agencies now
    audit whose knowledge frames their programmes, museums are legally restructuring
    acquisition policies, and courts have heard claims built on postcolonial historical
    scholarship — from Mau Mau compensation (Britain settled in 2013) to Chagos
    sovereignty. The theory changed what counts as evidence, and evidence changed what
    institutions owe.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The divergence they inherited.</strong> Why did some postcolonial states converge
    while others stalled? Singapore and South Korea — both poor, both post-colonial in 1960 —
    reached high income within two generations; commodity-rich states with comparable
    starting points did not. The honest literature refuses a single cause: initial conditions
    (settler institutions versus extraction colonies, human capital at independence), policy
    choices (export discipline versus import substitution), leadership tenure and sheer luck
    all appear in the winning cases — which is precisely why the "one secret of development"
    genre sells well and proves little.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The empire debate, quantified.</strong> Was empire, on balance, an engine of
    development or an extractor of it? Niall Ferguson's case for British investments,
    railways and common law met a wall of quantified counter-evidence: deindustrialisation of
    Indian textiles, famines under laissez-faire (the Bengal famine of 1943 killed around
    three million while food moved in regulated markets), and the extractive-institutions
    econometrics that link colonial labour regimes to lower modern incomes. The question is
    no longer only historiographical — it drives reparations claims, museum restitution and
    treaty litigation, making decolonisation one of the few fields of history where the
    footnotes end up in court, and the audited archives increasingly end up in public.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The unfinished agenda.</strong> The last colonial roster is short but stubborn —
    Western Sahara, the Chagos archipelago (whose 2019 ICJ opinion against the UK showed the
    law still moving), and the French overseas departments' ambiguous status. Meanwhile the
    successor issues — who owns extracted data and green-transition minerals, whose museums
    hold whose heritage, how migration routes follow colonial ones — are the same debates in
    new materials. Postcolonial history, in other words, is not a completed chapter; it is
    the operating manual of the present, and its final sections have not been written.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Said, Orientalism (1978); Fanon, The Wretched of the Earth (1961); Acemoglu & Robinson on colonial institutions; UN decolonisation records.</p>
